# Rustty-only Valgrind suppressions for G-VALGRIND (check_valgrind.sh), kept
# apart from `valgrind.supp`, which stays the reference's file verbatim.
#
# Scope: Mesa's llvmpipe and EGL, the software OpenGL driver and display
# layer the renderer's live-GL tests run on (LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1, RUSTTY_REQUIRE_GL=1). The reference's
# `valgrind.supp` hides graphics-driver noise the same way, by naming the
# driver's own frames (its "Mesa", "Graphics Driver dri", "i965 addr4/addr8" and
# "mesa memcpy/memcmp" entries). The reference runs no test that draws, so it
# has no llvmpipe entries; these are the port's, one group per finding
# (NEW-FINDINGS N412, N509), each measured on the Fedora 44 aarch64 VM (Mesa 26.1.4,
# LLVM 22.1.8, valgrind 3.27.1).

# llvmpipe compiles its shaders with LLVM (gallivm) at draw time; memcheck
# reports conditional jumps inside LLVM's code generator (AsmPrinter,
# SelectionDAG lowering) on a value created by a stack allocation in Mesa's
# `lp_build_create_jit_compiler_for_module`. Reproduced by
# `.port/tools/valgrind-llvmpipe-probe/probe.c` (C, EGL surfaceless, no Rustty
# code at all), and by a single-triangle variant of it, so it is
# driver-internal. Every frame between the report and
# `gallivm_jit_function` belongs to LLVM or Mesa; a Rustty defect cannot be the
# top of such a stack. The stack is deeper than valgrind's default of 12
# callers: the entry matches only with the gate's `--num-callers=50` (review
# L-6).
{
   llvmpipe-gallivm-llvm-codegen
   Memcheck:Cond
   ...
   fun:gallivm_jit_function
}

# No entry for the golden tests' read-back pixels. llvmpipe's blending makes
# memcheck mark some of them undefined although each holds its expected value
# (N412, probe.c); the two tests that branch on them declare exactly those
# bytes defined with `std.valgrind.memcheck.makeMemDefined`
# (`rustty_zigstd::valgrind::memcheck::make_mem_defined`) right after
# `read_rgba()`, so every other value they branch on stays checked. Until
# review L-6 F2 five entries here anchored on the test bodies instead, which
# also hid conditional jumps on renderer state.

# Mesa EGL's driver state for a display that is never terminated (N509).
# The port, like the reference, never calls eglTerminate on the shared
# surfaceless display (`OpenGL.zig:113-115`, "Do not destroy the EGL display
# here as it is shared across the entire process. It will get automatically
# torn down by the OS."; DECISIONS B10, `OffscreenContext`'s Drop). At process
# exit Mesa's own teardown of its display list frees each `_EGLDisplay` but not
# the driver data hung off an initialized one, so memcheck's end-of-process
# scan finds the dri2 display (`dri2_display_create`) and its config array
# (`_eglLinkConfig`) with no pointer left: "definitely lost". Reproduced with
# no Rustty code: a 10-line C program that calls eglGetPlatformDisplayEXT
# (surfaceless) + eglInitialize and returns reports exactly these two records
# (384 and 32 direct bytes); the same program with eglTerminate before return
# reports 0 errors (`.port/tools/valgrind-llvmpipe-probe/egl-no-terminate.c`).
# Both entries need every frame from the allocator up to
# `eglInitialize` to be Mesa's, and match `definite` leaks only. (LSan's
# twins: SANITIZER_SUPPRESSIONS.md S1/S2.)
{
   mesa-egl-dri2-display-not-terminated
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite
   fun:calloc
   fun:dri2_display_create
   fun:dri2_initialize
   ...
   fun:eglInitialize
}
# The config array of the same display; see the group comment above (N509).
{
   mesa-egl-dri2-configs-not-terminated
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite
   fun:calloc
   fun:_eglCreateArray
   fun:_eglLinkConfig
   fun:dri2_add_config
   ...
   fun:dri2_initialize
   ...
   fun:eglInitialize
}
